Allow a context before its surfaces

GStreamer's va decoders (vah264dec, vah265dec, vaav1dec, ...) create the
decode context with no render targets, before any surface exists, and
allocate surfaces on demand afterwards. VA-API allows this. This driver
did not, and every GStreamer va pipeline failed before decoding a frame:

  vadecoder: vaCreateContext: operation failed
  vah264dec: Failed to allocated output buffer, return not-negotiated
  vah264dec: No valid frames decoded before end of stream

(libva trace: vaCreateConfig, then vaCreateContext with
num_render_targets = 0, and nothing else.) The context needed the
capture format, which is set only when surfaces are created, and it
started both queues at once, which V4L2 refuses while no buffer exists.

A context created before any surface now starts unstreamed and starts
its queues at its first BeginPicture. By then the surfaces exist, and
with them the format and the buffers. Surface creation counts only a
streaming context as fixing the queue geometry, so the first surfaces
after an early context set the format instead of being treated as
auxiliary. Destroying a context that never streamed does not STREAMOFF.
Contexts created after their surfaces, which is what ffmpeg and mpv do,
behave exactly as before.

GStreamer also needs GST_VA_ALL_DRIVERS=1. It registers va elements only
for allow-listed drivers, and without the variable there are none for
v4l2_request. That is an environment setting, not a patch.

